#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079501} :activity/id #uuid "9b5429f3-219e-5e7d-8ead-04a47137510b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067 :topic/id #uuid "2a8497f4-0ed3-5e71-a11d-456426f348f7" :topic/math-academy-id 680} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079626 :tutorial/content "The general formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo illustrate how this works, suppose we want to find the $10$th term of the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is $r=2,$ so we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $10$th term, we can use this formula with $n={\\color{blue}10}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{10} & =3\\cdot {2}^{10-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{9} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 512 \\\\  & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is $a_{10}=1\\,536.$ Using the formula was much quicker than writing out all $10$ terms of the sequence!" :tutorial/id #uuid "703b282b-01c6-51a9-a283-add8f4090497" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1669 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c2d6289-df55-596a-b3a0-5806efcd1775" :step/math-academy-id 467473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078558 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078202 :question/id #uuid "f6a49cd7-9558-524c-b511-b13bd792cfc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-1509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01f44f8b-5db2-567a-89ce-9283f2f1b0c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050977}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550015}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of a Geometric Sequence Given the First Term and Common Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "656a41b2-9410-5567-bea2-804ce130538e" :step/math-academy-id 467474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078559 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078204 :question/id #uuid "4bf89b8f-f713-51b5-8536-5aa5b08f16d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5760a53a-1a0c-568e-ac9c-c820eaad3010" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559848}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Formula for the Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "b7caa67e-ec40-569e-8487-4dd0571a7625" :step/math-academy-id 467478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078560 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078203 :question/id #uuid "f7b26998-6137-54f9-bfe1-a2c3ba5eb0f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1510"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d82eb202-fec9-5769-a006-67d42b932303" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549999}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term of a Geometric Sequence Given a Term and the Common Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "893cc389-049a-5bf7-a0b1-f7ff35257fe5" :step/math-academy-id 467482}] :activity/title "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078695}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559963} :activity/id #uuid "387c0af8-04ff-5b91-acbd-ef00561b520e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067 :topic/id #uuid "2a8497f4-0ed3-5e71-a11d-456426f348f7" :topic/math-academy-id 680} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1381eca8-6c85-572d-a31b-e3bf882f85e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6635ce75-68d9-56d3-9e68-8210fcf2e22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-231757" :question/problem "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "17f20667-bb76-5a5f-90f5-ccb22eb38b24"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dced77a5-fd44-5940-8816-bb90dfa191d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(-3)^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559558}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559558} :answer-field/id #uuid "002196b5-3d0b-53c5-b861-202ffed83ecb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15178543-fce2-524a-bf78-a8e6e6bf0c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-231755" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $4$th term is $-135$ and the common ratio is $-3.$\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-3,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $4$th term is ${a}_{4}=-135,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{4-1} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-3)^{3} \\\\ -135 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-27) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5(-3)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e56d537-0b08-5372-bec2-2b132cbebf16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559293} {:answer/id #uuid "72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559304} {:answer/id #uuid "73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=1+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559306} {:answer/id #uuid "b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559465} {:answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470} :answer-field/id #uuid "27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :question/math-academy-id "q-5437" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b569b644-bc51-538e-91cf-d1aafe61dc67" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559914}}] :activity/title "The Nth Term of a Geometric Sequence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559001}]}
